-- title:
--   Finite unions preserve Lipschitz parametrization dimension
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a pseudo extended metric space, let $d\in\mathbb N$, and let $S,T\subseteq E$. A set is called $d$-dimensionally Lipschitz parametrizable if it is contained in a finite union of images of $[0,1]^d$ under Lipschitz maps. If both $S$ and $T$ have this property, then
--
--   $$
--   S\cup T\quad\text{is }d\text{-dimensionally Lipschitz parametrizable}.
--   $$
--
--   This allows separately parametrized boundary pieces of the same dimension to be combined.
